# FOCI Emissions Perturbation Analysis: CEDS vs EDGAR Comparison

[![Python](https://img.shields.io/badge/Python-3.8+-blue.svg)](https://www.python.org/)
[![License](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-yellow.svg)](LICENSE)
[![Jupyter](https://img.shields.io/badge/Jupyter-Notebook-orange.svg)](https://jupyter.org/)

**Quantifying discrepancies between CEDS and EDGAR emissions inventories to derive perturbations for CMIP6 climate scenarios**

---

## Overview

This repository contains a comprehensive analysis comparing two major global emissions inventories:

- **CEDS** (Community Emissions Data System) - v2024_04_01
- **EDGAR** (Emissions Database for Global Atmospheric Research) - v8.0/v8.1

The analysis covers **8 key pollutants** (CH₄, CO, CO₂, NH₃, NMVOC, NOx, SO₂, N₂O) over the **2004-2019** period, with a focus on the **energy sector**.

This work was conducted as part of the **FOCI** (Fostering Opportunities for Climate Innovation) project under **Horizon Europe**, contributing to improved understanding of non-CO₂ radiative forcers from energy production.

## Key Findings

### 1. Global Discrepancies
- **CEDS reports higher emissions** for CH₄, CO, NH₃, NMVOC, and N₂O
- **EDGAR reports higher emissions** for CO₂ (gap reached ~1.28 million kt by 2019)
- **CO and NOx** show early-period discrepancies that narrow over time

### 2. Energy Sector Focus
- **Oil and Natural Gas**: Largest discrepancies (CH₄: ±29% average, up to 33,311 kt in 2005)
- **Electricity and Heat Production**: Major discrepancies for SO₂ and NOx
- **Solid Fuels**: EDGAR reports higher emissions for CH₄ (China, Indonesia, India)

### 3. Top Emitting Countries
- **Russia, Venezuela, and China** account for majority of global discrepancies
- **Nigeria case study**: EDGAR shows anomalous CH₄ increase in 2019 requiring further investigation

### 4. Perturbations for Climate Scenarios
- Derived sector-specific percentage differences (2015-2019 average)
- Applied to CMIP6 SSP2-4.5 projections from MESSAGE-GLOBIOM
- Provides structural uncertainty estimates for climate modelling

